  <div class="wrap">
</div>

<div class="callout danger" style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto 20px;text-align:center;">A MAJOR DIVERGENCE FROM THE GLOBAL CONVERGENCE TREND: ASPE has not adopted a single on-balance-sheet lessee model. It retains the older dual classification approach (capital vs operating leases) for both lessees and lessors — standing in genuine contrast to IFRS 16, ASBE No. 21, and Japan's ASBJ No. 34, all of which moved lessees toward recognizing right-of-use assets and lease liabilities for virtually all leases.</div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">One of the most significant structural divergences in this entire poster library</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3065 governs LEASE accounting FOR both LESSEES and LESSORS — RETAINING the OLDER, dual-CLASSIFICATION approach (CAPITAL leases VERSUS operating LEASES) that PRE-DATES the GLOBAL shift TOWARD a SINGLE, on-BALANCE-sheet lessee MODEL. THIS represents ONE of the MOST significant STRUCTURAL divergences FROM the CONVERGENCE trend RUNNING throughout the REST of this ENTIRE poster LIBRARY (Section 3).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Agreements conveying the right to use an asset for a period in exchange for payment</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to LEASE agreements — CONTRACTS conveying the RIGHT to USE a SPECIFIC asset FOR a period OF time in EXCHANGE for PAYMENT — for BOTH the LESSEE and LESSOR sides OF such an ARRANGEMENT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>THE CENTRAL DIVERGENCE — Dual Classification, Not a Single On-Balance-Sheet Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Two categories, with genuinely different accounting outcomes</div><div class="text-supporting">EVERY lease is CLASSIFIED, at ITS inception, as EITHER a CAPITAL lease OR an OPERATING lease — a CAPITAL lease is RECOGNIZED on the LESSEE'S own BALANCE Sheet (an ASSET and a CORRESPONDING liability); an OPERATING lease is NOT recognized on the BALANCE Sheet AT all, WITH lease PAYMENTS simply EXPENSED as INCURRED. THIS stands IN stark CONTRAST to IFRS 16, ASBE No. 21, and JAPAN'S ASBJ No. 34 (see the COMPANION posters ELSEWHERE in this LIBRARY), ALL of WHICH require LESSEES to RECOGNIZE a right-OF-use asset and LEASE liability for VIRTUALLY all LEASES, REGARDLESS of CLASSIFICATION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Capital Lease Classification Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Transferring substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Ownership of the asset transfers to the lessee by the end of the lease term</span><span>The lease contains a bargain purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise</span><span>The lease term is for the major part of the asset's remaining economic life</span><span>The present value of minimum lease payments amounts to substantially all of the asset's fair value at inception</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Lessee Accounting for a Capital Lease</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">An asset and liability at the present value of minimum lease payments</div><div class="text-supporting">A LESSEE recognizes a CAPITAL lease as BOTH an ASSET (RECORDED at the LOWER of the ASSET'S fair VALUE and the PRESENT value OF minimum LEASE payments) and a CORRESPONDING lease OBLIGATION — the ASSET is SUBSEQUENTLY depreciated (PER Section 3061'S own PRINCIPLES), and the LEASE obligation is REDUCED as PAYMENTS are MADE, WITH an IMPUTED interest COMPONENT recognized AS an EXPENSE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Lessee Accounting for an Operating Lease</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Simply expensed, with no balance sheet recognition</div><div class="text-supporting">A LESSEE under an OPERATING lease SIMPLY recognizes LEASE payments as an EXPENSE, GENERALLY on a straight-LINE basis OVER the LEASE term — WITH NO asset OR liability RECOGNIZED on the BALANCE Sheet WHATSOEVER, REGARDLESS of the LEASE'S total COMMITMENT or DURATION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Lessor Accounting — a Parallel Dual Classification</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Sales-type, direct financing, or operating leases</div><div class="text-supporting">A LESSOR similarly CLASSIFIES each LEASE — as a SALES-type lease (WHERE the LESSOR is EFFECTIVELY the MANUFACTURER or DEALER, recognizing a PROFIT at LEASE commencement), a DIRECT financing LEASE (WHERE the LESSOR is EFFECTIVELY financing the ASSET'S acquisition, WITHOUT a manufacturer'S PROFIT), or an OPERATING lease (RETAINING the ASSET on ITS OWN balance SHEET, recognizing LEASE income OVER the lease TERM).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Why ASPE Retained the Older Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Cost-benefit considerations for typically simpler private-enterprise leasing arrangements</div><div class="text-supporting">THE global SHIFT toward a SINGLE on-BALANCE-sheet lessee MODEL (IFRS 16, ASC 842, ASBJ No. 34) was LARGELY motivated BY enhancing COMPARABILITY for PUBLIC capital MARKETS investors, WHO had HISTORICALLY struggled TO compare enterprises USING extensive OFF-balance-sheet OPERATING leases AGAINST those FINANCING similar ASSETS through ON-balance-sheet DEBT. FOR private enterprises, WHOSE primary USERS (LENDERS, tax AUTHORITIES, owner-MANAGERS) OFTEN already ADJUST for OPERATING lease COMMITMENTS through OTHER means (e.g. DIRECT inquiry, NOTE disclosure REVIEW), ASPE'S standard-SETTERS concluded the SUBSTANTIAL implementation COST of adopting a SINGLE model WOULD likely EXCEED its INCREMENTAL benefit TO this NARROWER user BASE — a DELIBERATE, cost-CONSCIOUS choice TO retain the OLDER, more FAMILIAR dual-CLASSIFICATION approach.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Classifying and Accounting for a Capital Lease</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">On-balance-sheet recognition, given the lease's genuine ownership-like nature</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A lease covering substantially all of an asset's economic life</div><div class="wk-body">A company leases specialized equipment for 8 years, matching the equipment's full 8-year estimated economic life, with the present value of minimum lease payments equal to $480,000 (the equipment's approximate fair value).</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN the LEASE term COVERS the MAJOR part OF the asset'S economic LIFE, and the PRESENT value OF payments APPROXIMATES fair VALUE, the LEASE is CLASSIFIED as a <strong>CAPITAL lease</strong> — the COMPANY recognizes a $480,000 ASSET and a CORRESPONDING $480,000 LEASE obligation on ITS Balance SHEET.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — an Operating Lease, Off Balance Sheet</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">No balance sheet recognition whatsoever, regardless of total commitment</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A short-term office space lease</div><div class="wk-body">A company leases office space for 3 years, representing a small fraction of the building's overall economic life, with no ownership transfer, bargain purchase option, or payments approximating the property's fair value. Total lease payments over the term are $360,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN NONE of the CAPITAL lease CRITERIA are MET, the LEASE is CLASSIFIED as an <strong>OPERATING lease</strong> — the COMPANY simply EXPENSES $360,000 ÷ 3 = $120,000 per YEAR, with <strong>NO asset OR liability</strong> recognized ON the Balance SHEET at ALL.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3065 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature and terms of significant lease arrangements</span><span>Carrying amount of capital lease assets, and the related obligation, by major class</span><span>Future minimum lease payments under both capital and operating leases, for each of the next several years</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a lease meets the capital lease classification criteria, particularly the "major part" and "substantially all" thresholds</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Discount rate used to calculate the present value of minimum lease payments</span><span>Asset's remaining economic life and fair value at lease inception</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS 16</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3065 vs IFRS 16</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3065</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 16</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Lessee model</span><span>Dual classification — capital vs operating</span><span>SINGLE on-balance-sheet model for virtually all leases</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Operating lease balance sheet impact</span><span>NONE</span><span>Right-of-use asset and lease liability recognized</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">ONE of the MOST significant, PRACTICALLY consequential DIVERGENCES this ENTIRE poster LIBRARY has DOCUMENTED — ASPE'S retained DUAL model STANDS as a GENUINE, deliberate DEPARTURE from the GLOBAL convergence TREND toward on-BALANCE-sheet lease ACCOUNTING.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every lease event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Capital lease recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Asset and obligation recognized at lease inception</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Operating lease payments</span><span><span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Expensed only; no Balance Sheet impact</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3061</span><span>Property, Plant and Equipment — supplies the depreciation principles applied to a capital lease asset</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Capital lease</div><div class="glossary-def">A lease transferring substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership, recognized as an asset and liability on the lessee's Balance Sheet.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Operating lease</div><div class="glossary-def">A lease not meeting the capital lease criteria, with payments simply expensed and no Balance Sheet recognition.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3065 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
